== The Third Sermon

#quote[On this mountain the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts will make for all people a feast. And He will destroy on this mountain the face of the covering cast over all people, and the veil that is spread over all nations.—Isa 25:6, 7.]

I have spoken before of the feast that God makes for his church, especially in the latter times. It was especially fulfilled at the first coming of Christ, when the Gentiles came in; but the consummation and perfection of all will be at the day of judgment. Then God will spread a table for his own to all eternity.

We have spoken before at length of how spiritual good things are set out by this comparison of a feast. God sets out spiritual things by outward ones, because we cannot otherwise conceive of them; the best things in grace by the best and sweetest things in nature. And so God enters into our souls by our senses, as we see in the sacrament.

But we have spoken at length of this. Our care must be to have a special taste, a spiritual appetite to relish this feast that God provides. By nature we are without taste for it. We do not relish spiritual and heavenly things; we do not savor the things of God. And the Spirit of God must alter our savor and taste, as he does. Wherever there is spiritual life, there is a spiritual relish of heavenly truths.

Now let me add this further: though the feast is made by God, yet we must bring something to it. Christ feasts with us, as you#super[+] have it in Rev 3:20. He sups with us. It is not that we have grace from ourselves, or can bring anything; he brings his own provision with him when he sups with us. But yet, by the covenant of grace, by which he enters into terms of friendship with us, we must sup with him; we must have grace to welcome him, though it is at his own cost. Yet we must have something. He does not require us to pay our debts, but he gives us that with which to pay them. Secretly he bids us come, but he gives a secret messenger to draw us; he certainly sends his Spirit. Certainly he will have us bring something when we come to feast, but it is of his own giving. And what we are to bring is humble and empty souls, in which we are to delight ourselves in the sense of our unworthiness; and the spirit of faith to believe his promises. That pleases him, when we can honor him with a spirit of faith; and then a spirit of love, and new obedience springing from a spirit of faith and love. These are the things Christ requires us to have. Our souls must be furnished in this way, so that Christ may delight to dwell with us. And therefore it is a good thing to plead earnestly with God: “Lord, I desire that you would dwell in me, and prepare my soul as a fit temple”; grant me the graces you delight in, and delight to dwell in. So we may beg of God his Holy Spirit to furnish our souls, so that he may dwell and delight in us.

But we have spoken at length of the former verse. I will now speak of the next one that follows.

“And He will destroy on this mountain the face of the covering cast over all people, and the veil that is spread over all nations. He will swallow up death in victory, and the Lord will wipe away tears from all faces, and the rebukes of His people He will take away from the earth; for the #smallcaps[Lord] has spoken it.”

These depend on one another, being the several courses of the feast. He promises a feast in the sixth verse. And what are its several courses? He will destroy on this mountain, this church, the face of the covering cast over all people. He will take away the veil of ignorance and unbelief, so that they may have a special sight of heavenly things. Without it they cannot relish heavenly things; they can take no joy at this feast.

And then, because there can be no feast where the greatest enemy is in force and power, he swallows up death in victory. Death keeps us in fear all our lifetime. That which swallows up all kings and monarchs, the terror of the world, death, will be and is swallowed up by our head, Christ, and will be swallowed up by us in victory. In the meantime we are subject to many sorrows which cause tears; for tears are only drops that fall from that cloud of sorrow. And sorrow we always have in this world, either from our sins or our miseries, or from sympathy, in tears of that kind. Well, the time will come when tears will be wiped away, and the cause of tears: all sorrow for our own sins, for our own misery, and from sympathizing with the times in which we live. Our time for this will be hereafter, at the day of the resurrection, when all tears will be wiped from our eyes. God will perform that office of a mother, to wipe the children’s eyes, or of a nurse, to take away all cause of grief whatever; otherwise it could not be a perfect feast.

Yes, but there are reproaches cast on religion and on religious people! Religion goes under a veil of reproach, and the best things are not seen in their own colors; nor are the worst things. They go under masks here.

But the time will come when the rebukes of his people will be taken away. The good things, as they are best, so will they be known to be so; and sin and base courses, as they are bad and as they come from hell, so they will be known to be. Everything will appear in its own colors; things will not go masked any longer. And what is the seal of all this? The seal of it is, “The mouth of the #smallcaps[Lord] has spoken it.” Truth itself has spoken it, and therefore it must necessarily be. Jehovah, who can give being to all things, has said it. We have heard why the church is called a mountain. He will destroy, or swallow up, as the word may signify, the face of covering, or the covering of the face; the veil, which is the covering of the face, and which that term always particularly expresses; the veil that is spread over all nations.

God will take away the spiritual veil that covers the souls of his people, which is between them and divine truths. It alludes to Exodus 34:34, 35, to Moses when he came down from the mount. He had a veil, for the people could not look at him. He had a glory put on his face, so that they could not look on him with a direct eye; and therefore he had to put a veil over his face, to show that the Jews could not see, as Paul interprets it (2 Cor 3:15): “To this day,” he says, “when Moses is read, a veil covers their hearts.” They could not see that “the law was a guardian to lead to Christ” (Gal 3:24), the ceremonial law and the moral law. God had a blessed end, by the curse of the law, to bring them to Christ. They rested in the veil; their sight ended at the veil; they could not see through to its end and purpose. Nevertheless, when they turn to the Lord, the veil will be taken away.

+ From the words, consider first of all that by nature there is a veil of ignorance on the soul.
+ Secondly, God takes away his veil; and only God by his Spirit can do it.
+ Thirdly, this is only in his church. And where this veil of ignorance is taken off, there is feasting with God, and spiritual joy, and delight in the best manner; and where it is not taken off, there is none of it. First of all, by nature there is a veil of covering over all men’s spirits. To understand this better, let us unfold the terms of the veil a little. There is a veil either on the things themselves that are to be seen, or on the soul which should see them.

(1.) The veil on the things themselves is when they are hidden altogether, or in part; when we know part and are ignorant of part. And this veil on the things arises from the weak apprehension of them: when they are not set out in clear expressions, but in obscure words or in types; when we see them only in types or obscure phrases, which sometimes hide the sight of the thing itself. The manner of speech sometimes casts a veil on things. For our Savior Christ spoke in parables, which were like the cloud, dark on the one side and light on the other: dark toward the Egyptians, light toward the Israelites. So some expressions of Scripture have a light side, which only the godly see, and a dark side, which other men of good intelligence, such as natural men, do not see.

(2.) Again, there is a veil on the soul and on the sight. If the things are veiled, or the sight is veiled, there is no sight. Now the soul is veiled when we are ignorant and unbelieving: when we are ignorant of what is spoken and revealed, or when we know its terms and yet do not believe it.

(3.) Now, this veil of ignorance and unbelief continues in all unregenerate men until grace takes away the veil. Besides, before a thing can be seen, not only must the object be made clear, and the eyesight too, but there must be lumen deferens, a light to carry the object to the eye. If that is lacking, we cannot see. So the Egyptians, in the three days of darkness, had their eyes, but they lacked light to show them the object, and therefore they could not go near one another. It is the light, and not the sight. If there is sight and no light to carry and convey the object, we cannot say there is sight. What answers to this veil is the veil of Scripture, by which heavenly things are set out in a mystery. A mystery is when something is openly shown and something hidden.

When something is concealed, as in the sacrament, they are mysteries. We see the bread, we see the wine, but under the bread and wine other things are meant: the breaking of the body of Christ and the shedding of his blood, and in that the love and mercy of God in Christ, in giving him to death for us, and Christ’s love in giving himself to satisfy divine justice. These are the things meant, which only the soul sees and grasps. And so all things in the church are indeed mysteries: the incarnation of Christ; the union of both natures; that Christ should save the world in such a way as he did; that he should bring us to glory by shame, to life by death, to blessing and happiness by being a curse for us. It is a mystery to bring contrary out of contrary: that so glorious a person as God should be covered with our weak and sinful nature. It was a mystery; the Jews stumbled at it. Light came, and the darkness could not comprehend the light. And as Christ was a mystery himself, so the church is a mystery. That God should delight so much in a company of poor men, the refuse of the world, to make them temples of his Holy Spirit and heirs of heaven, men who were under the scorn of the world: this is a mystery. So all is mystical: the head, the members, the body, the church, and every particular point of religion. There is a mystery in repentance. No man knows what sorrow for sin is except the truly gracious person. No man knows what it is to believe except he who has a heart to believe. No man knows what peace of conscience and joy of the Holy Spirit are except those who feel them. So that is a mystery. And therefore “the mystery of godliness is great,” says the apostle (1 Tim 3:16). Not only in the points themselves, but even the practice of religion is a mystery too. Repentance and faith, and new obedience and love, and the comforts of religion, are all mysteries. There is a veil on them in all these points, so that a carnal man cannot see them.

You#super[+] see, then, in what sense there is a veil on the things, and in what sense there is a veil on men’s hearts: that is, either the things themselves are hidden, or, if the things are open, men lack the sight and light of knowledge, and they lack faith to believe. Beloved, we live in times when the object is clear to us; the things themselves are made clear. Who does not know what Christ is, and the notion of the incarnation, and of union with him? We know them as notions. They are opened and revealed to us very clearly, all the articles of faith and mysteries of religion, so that there is no obscurity in the object. The things are clear, especially in these places of knowledge. But yet, for all that, there is a veil on the soul. The soul of every man who is not graciously worked on by the Spirit of God has a veil of ignorance and unbelief.

First of all, of ignorance. There is a veil of ignorance in many, and in all men by nature a veil of ignorance of spiritual things. For unless they are revealed, they can never be known even by the angels themselves. The angels themselves do not know the gospel until it is opened, and therefore they are continually students of it; and the best men in the world know nothing in the gospel further than it is revealed. But there is a veil of ignorance on those who know these things in notion only, because they do not know them as they should know them. They do not know them in propria specie [in their own proper form], spiritual and heavenly things as spiritual and heavenly things. They do not know spiritual things as spiritual things; they have a human knowledge of spiritual things. Those who lack grace know the grammar of the Scripture and of divinity, and they know how to reason as the schoolmen do, from one thing to another, and to argue. They know the logic and rhetoric of the Scripture, but they stick at the style. There is something they are ignorant of; that is, they do not have an eye of knowledge, as we call it. They do not see the things themselves, but only see things by another person’s spirit, and they have no light of their own. And so no man by nature, except the children of God, knows what original sin is, what corruption of nature is, nor knows sin in its own odious colors, as filthy and as dangerous as it is. That it draws the curse and vengeance of God upon it is not known except by the Spirit revealing the odiousness of sin, so that the soul may apprehend it as Christ did when he suffered for it, and as God does. A gracious man sees it as God sees it, because by the Spirit of God he sees its filthiness and odiousness, and the danger it draws after it.

Second. And so in any points of religion, a man by nature does not see them spiritually, as they are and as God sees them, but he sees them by a human light. He sees heavenly things by a human light, in notion only, and only to talk of them. He does not see intuitively into the things themselves. He sees them sub aliena specie, under another representation than their own. Only a godly man sees spiritual things as the Spirit of God sees them, and sees them as they are: he knows sin as it is, knows grace as it is, and knows faith, what it is to believe, what it is to have peace of conscience and the pardon of sins. He knows these things in some sense intuitively, though not as he will when he sees these things in heaven, when he sees face to face. There is a great difference in it. He sees them intuitively in respect of the knowledge of other men, though he sees only as in a glass in regard of the knowledge he will have in heaven. As St Paul says, “For we see but as in a glass.” But he who sees in a glass sees more life than he who sees the dead picture of a man. So, though we see heavenly things only in a glass, yet we see them better than those who see them in a dead notion. Though it is nothing to the knowledge we will have in heaven, yet it is incomparably above the knowledge of any carnal, natural man on the earth.

Third. Again, by nature men have veils of ignorance over the most divine things: over spiritual things, such as union, and the communion between Christ and us, and the mystery of regeneration in the new creature, such as joy in the Holy Spirit and inward peace of conscience. I will not name the particulars to dwell on them, but give you#super[+] only an instance. Though they know the notion of these things, yet they are altogether ignorant of them. Their knowledge is a sheer outward light. The gracious man’s is a light rooted in the soul. It is not like the light of the moon, which receives light from the sun, but it is a light rooted and incorporated into the soul by the Spirit, as the light of the sun is. It is in the soul. It is not only on the soul, but in the soul. The heart sees and feels and knows divine truths. There is a power and force in the sight and knowledge of a gracious man; there is none in the knowledge of a carnal man. The light of a candle has light in it, but no force at all goes with it; but the light of the sun and the light of the stars have a special power: they have heat with them, and they have an influence of a special kind on the bodies below, working together with the light. So it is with heavenly apprehension and knowledge. It actually conveys light. But with the light there is a blessed and gracious influence; there is heat and efficacy with that light. But though a carnal man knows the whole body of divinity, yet it is a sheer light without heat, a light without influence. It is not from experience. A blind man, if he is a scholar, can talk of colors and describe them better than one who has his eyes but is not a scholar. But he who has his eyes can judge of colors a great deal better. Often a scholar can tell you#super[+] about foreign countries from books better than one who has traveled, yet the traveler who has been there can tell of them more distinctly. So one who is experienced in that kind of work, though a stranger, can measure another man’s ground better than the owner himself. He can tell you#super[+] there are so many acres here. But he who possesses them knows their goodness and their worth, and cultivates them for his own good. And so it is with many. They can measure the points of religion, and define and divide them. Yes, but the poor Christian can taste them, feel them, relish them and put them to use. His knowledge is a knowledge with a share in the things, but other men’s knowledge is a knowledge with no share or experience at all. So there is by nature a veil of ignorance on the heart of every natural man.

Christianity is a mystery. Until conversion there is a mystery in every point of religion. None know what repentance is except a repentant sinner. All the books in the world cannot inform the heart what sin is or what sorrow is. A sick man knows what a disease is better than all the physicians, for he feels it. No man knows what faith is except the true believer. There is a mystery also in love. Godliness is called a mystery, not only for its notional part, but for its practical part. Why do men not take more delight in the transcendent things of religion, which may enrapture angels? Alas! there is a veil over their souls, so that they do not know them, or do not know them by experience. They have no taste or feeling of them.

And so there is a veil of unbelief. There is no man without grace who truly believes what he knows; he believes only in general, he believes things only so far as they do not cross his lusts. But when particular truths are pressed on a carnal man, his lusts overbear all his knowledge, and a secret scorn rises in his heart, by which he derides those truths and goes against them; and it makes him think that certainly these things are not true, and therefore he does not believe them. If a man by nature believed the truths he says he knows, he would not go directly against them. But the ground of this is that a mist of sinful lusts is raised out of the soul which darkens the soul, so that at that time the soul is atheistic and full of unbelief. For there is no sin but ignorance and unbelief breathe it into the soul and make way for it; for if a man knew what he was doing, and understood that God saw him, and the danger of it, he would never sin.

There is no sin without an error in judgment; there is a veil of ignorance and unbelief. What creature will run into a pit when it sees it open? What creature, even the dullest, will run into the fire? A man will not run into a danger that is open to the eye of the soul, if there is not a veil of ignorance, or at least of unbelief, on the soul at that time. All sin presupposes error.

And this should make us hate sin the more. Whenever we sin, especially against our conscience, there is atheism in the soul at that time, and there is unbelief. We do not believe truth itself. There is no sinner who does not call truth into question. When he sins, he denies it or questions it; and therefore there is a veil over every man’s heart by nature, through ignorance and unbelief. The truths themselves are clear. God is clear, and the gospel is light, mens, lux [mind, light]; you#super[+] know, they know things in the object, but in us there is darkness in our understandings. And therefore the Scripture says not only that we are dark, but that we are “darkness” itself (Eph 5:8). The clouds that arise are like the mists that come between our souls and divine things, arising from our own hearts; and the love of sinful things raises such a cloud that we do not know, or else do not believe, what is spoken. To proceed.

Observation 2. God alone can reveal and take away the veil of ignorance and unbelief from the soul. I will speak especially of this veil.

Reason 1. The reason is that there is such a natural unsuitableness between the soul and heavenly light and heavenly truths that, unless God opens the eye of the soul and puts a new eye into the soul, it can never know or discern heavenly things. There must be an eye suited to the light, or there will never be sight of it. Now God can create a new spiritual eye to discern spiritual things, which a natural eye cannot. Who can see things invisible? Divine things are invisible to natural eyes. There is no suitableness. He who is to reveal these and take away the veil must create a new light within as well as a light without. Now God, and only God, who created light out of darkness, can create light in the soul: “Let there be light.” He alone can create a spiritual eye, to see the things that to nature are visible.

There are four things in sight:

+ The object to be seen.
+ The light that conveys it.
+ The organ that receives it.
+ And the light of the eye to meet the light without.

So it is in the soul. Together with divine truths, there must be light to reveal them; for light is the first visible thing, which reveals itself and all other things. And then there must be a light in the soul to judge of them, and this light must be suited to them. A carnal, base spirit judges spiritual things carnally, like himself, because he has no light in his own spirit. The things are spiritual; his eye is carnal. He has no light in his eye suited to the object, and therefore he cannot judge of them; for the Scripture says plainly, “they are spiritually discerned” (1 Cor 2:14). Therefore a carnal person has carnal conceptions of spiritual things, as a holy man spiritualizes things by a spiritual conception of them. There are degrees of discerning things. The highest degree is to see things “face to face,” as they are in heaven. The next to that is to see them in a glass; for there I see the motion and true image of a man, though not so clearly as when I see him face to face. Therefore we soon forget the image of it in a glass; we keep a more fixed image of the other, because there is more reality in it. We see things put into water, and that is less; but then there is a sight of a man in pictures, which is less than the rest, because we do not see the motion. It is just so: a carnal man scarcely sees the dead likeness of things. In Moses’s time they saw things as in water, dimly as it were, though truly; but we see things in a glass of truth as clearly as we possibly can in this world. In heaven we will see face to face; we will see him as he is. And then will be the joy of this excellent feast, and the consummation of all the sweet promises, of which here we can have only a taste.

Reason 2. So that is the first reason why God alone takes away the veil, which arises from the unsuitableness between the soul and divine truths.

There is nothing in the heart of man but a contrariety to divine light. Even natural knowledge is contrary to it. Natural conscience checks only for gross sins, not for spiritual sins. Obedience and a civil life make a man full of pride, and arm him against self-denial and against the righteousness of Christ and justification. There is nothing in the soul, without grace, that does not rise against the soul in divine things. Reason 3. Again, there is such a disproportion between the soul, being full of sin and guilt, and heavenly things, which are so great, that the heart of man will not believe unless God convinces the soul that God is so good and gracious that, though these things are great and excellent, yet he will bestow them on our souls. And therefore he sends the Spirit, who overpowers the soul, though it is full of the fear and guilt that sin contracts. Though we are ever so unworthy, he will magnify his grace to poor sinners; and without that the soul will never believe, there is such an infinite disproportion between the soul and the things, between the sinful soul and the Spirit; so God must overpower the soul to make it believe. The Scripture is full of this. As we are by nature ignorant and full of unbelief, so God alone can overpower the soul and take away the veil of ignorance.

Reason 4. All the angels in heaven, and all the creatures in the world, the most skillful men in the world, cannot bring light into the soul; they cannot bring light into the heart. They can speak of divine things, but they understand them little. But to bring light into the heart, so that the heart may taste them and yield obedience to believe, that they cannot do. And therefore all God’s children are theodidactoi, taught by God. God alone has the privilege to teach the heart, to bend and bow the heart to believe. So God alone, by his Spirit, takes away the veil of ignorance and unbelief.

Observation 3. Now, the third thing is that this is peculiar to the church and to the children of God, to have the veil taken off. “On this mountain,” says the Scripture, “the veil of all faces will be swallowed up or taken away.”

I partly showed in the former point that it is peculiar to God’s children to have the veil taken off. There is a veil in all things. Either the things are hidden from men, as among the Gentiles, or, if the things are revealed, there is a veil on the heart. Their lusts raise up a cloud, and until God subdues them by the Holy Spirit they are dark, indeed darkness itself. Only Goshen had light when all Egypt was in darkness; so there is light only in the church, and all other parts of the world are in darkness. And among men in the church there is darkness on the souls of unregenerate men, who are not sanctified and subdued by the Spirit of God. And all godly men are full of light; indeed, they are “lights in the world” (Phil 2:15). As wicked men are darkness, so gracious men are made lights of the world by the Spirit of God, from him who is the true light, Christ himself.

It is peculiar to the church to know the greatest good and the greatest evil. It is nowhere but in the church, who are the people of God. None but God’s elect can know the greatest evil, that is, sin, which the Spirit of God reveals; and the greatest good, that is, God’s mercy in Christ, and sanctifying grace. The same Spirit does both. As light reveals foul things as well as fair, so the same Spirit of God reveals the loathsomeness of sin and the sweetness of grace. Where the one is, there is never the other; where there is not truly a deep discerning of sin, there is never knowledge of grace; there is none but in the church. Those who have the spirit of illumination have sanctification as well.

We will make use of all these together. You#super[+] see, then, what we are by nature, and that God’s grace must take away the veil; and this is from all those within the church, and in the church from those whom God is pleased to sanctify.

Observation. In the fourth place, where this veil is taken off from anyone, there is spiritual joy and feasting with it, as here he joins them both together.

“I will make a feast of fat things, and will take away the veil” (verse 7).

Reason. The reason for the connection of this is that the same Spirit who is a Spirit of revelations is a Spirit of comfort; and the same Spirit who is the Spirit of comfort is a Spirit of revelation. All the sweetness that the soul relishes comes from light, and all light that is spiritual conveys sweetness, both together. Beloved, there is a marvelous sweetness in divine truths. In Christ is all marrow; and in religion there is forgiveness of sins, and inward peace, and joy, and grace fitting us to be like Christ, and for heaven. They are incredibly sweet; they are all marrow. Yes, but they are so only to those who know them. Now God’s Spirit, who reveals these things to us, breeds a taste in the soul. The Spirit of illumination to God’s children is a Spirit of sanctification as well; and that sanctification alters the taste and relish of the will and affections, so that with the revealing of these things there is a taste and relish of them. It is sapida scientia, a savory knowledge, that they have. And therefore where he makes a feast, he takes away the veil; and where he takes away the veil, he makes a feast. What a wonderful satisfaction the soul has, when the veil is taken off, to see God reconciled in Christ! to see sin pardoned! to see the beginnings of grace, which will be finished and accomplished in glory! to discern that “peace which passes understanding” (Phil 4:7). What a marvelous sweetness there is in these things!

They cannot be spiritually revealed to the knowledge without a feast in the soul, in which the soul delights itself; so both of these go together.

And therefore we should not rest in a revealing that does not bring a savor with it to the soul. Undoubtedly, the knowledge that is not by divine revelation of heavenly truths has no solace and comfort for the soul.

We see the dependence of these on one another. Then let us make this use of all:

Use 1. Since there is a veil over all men by nature, the work of ignorance and unbelief; and since God alone takes it away by his Holy Spirit; and since only those who are godly and sanctified have it taken off; and since, where this is done, there is a spiritual feast, joy and comfort and strength: then let us labor to have this veil taken off. Let us labor to have the eyes of our understanding enlightened, to have our hearts subdued to believe; let us take notice of our natural condition. We are drowned and wrapped in darkness, the best of us all. It is not having knowledge of what we are by nature, it is not just any knowledge, that can bring us to heaven; there must be a revelation, a taking away of the veil. How many content themselves with the common light of education, and knowledge handed down! So they were brought up and catechized, and sat under such a ministry! But for spiritual knowledge of spiritual things, how little it is cared for! And yet this is necessary to salvation. There is great occasion to press this, that we do not rest in common knowledge. If religion is not known effectually, it is like lightning, which does not direct a man in his way, but dazzles him and puts him quite out of his way. Many have flashes of knowledge that affect them a little; but this affection is soon gone, and does not direct them a whit in the ways of life. And therefore labor that the will and affections may be subject. Beg of God a “heart of flesh” (2 Cor 3:3), a heart yielding to the truth. We know that truths which reach only the ear will harden, as none is harder than a common, formal Christian. A man had better fall into the hands of papists than into the hands of a formal, hypocritical Christian. Why? They pride themselves in their profession. There were no persecutors worse than the scribes and Pharisees, who stood in their own light. They were more cruel than Pilate. And therefore if we are informed, but not truly transformed, to love the truth we know and hate the evil we know, it makes us worse.

And then it enrages men the more. The more they know, the more they are enraged. When truths are pressed which men do not intend to obey, they fret against the ordinance, and cast stones, as it were, in the face of truth. When medicine stirs up bad humors but is not strong enough to carry them away, it endangers the body; and where light is not strong enough to dispel corruption when it stirs corruption up, it enrages it. When men know truth and are not molded into it, they first rage against it, and then little by little fall from it and become extreme enemies to it. It is a dangerous thing, therefore, to rest in naked knowledge. Beg of God, then, that he would take away the veil of ignorance and unbelief, so that light and life may go together; and so we will be fit to feast with the Lord.

Means. Now, so that we may have true saving knowledge, first, we must attend meekly on God’s ordinances, which are sanctified to this end, to let light into the soul.

+ If we want to know sin and our state by nature, and how to come out of it, then together with this revelation there must come a heavenly strength into the soul, a heavenly taste and relish. And therefore attend on the ordinances, and labor for a humble soul, empty of ourselves; and do not think to break into heavenly things by strength of natural abilities. God must reveal; God must take away the veil, and only by his Holy Spirit in the ordinance. The veil is taken away from the object in the opening of truths; but the veil must be taken away from the object, and from the heart too. There must be knowledge of the object, as well as an object. The object must be sanctified and fitted to the persons, or divine truths will never be understood divinely, nor spiritual truths spiritually. Labor to be emptied of yourselves#super[+]. In whatever measure we are emptied of our self-conceit and our own understanding, we are filled in divine things. In whatever measure we are emptied of ourselves, we are filled with the Spirit of God, and knowledge, and grace. As a vessel, in whatever measure it is emptied, is in that measure fit to be filled with more liquid poured in on top, so in whatever measure we grow in self-denial and humility, in that measure we are also filled with knowledge. He will teach a humble soul that does not stand in its own light what it is to repent, to believe, to love; what it is to be patient under the cross; what it is to live holily and die with comfort. The Spirit of God will teach a humble, self-denying soul all these things. And therefore labor for a humble, empty soul, and not to cast ourselves too much into the sins and fashions of the times, as the apostle says: “Do not be conformed to this world, but be transformed by the renewing of your#super[+] mind” (Rom 12:2).
