  + When a man casts himself into the mold of the times, and will live as the rest do, he will never understand the secrets of God and the good pleasure of God; for the world must be condemned. The world goes the broad way. And therefore we must not consider what others do, but what God teaches us to do.
  + And add to this: what we know, let us labor to practice. “But he who does the will of My Father will know of every teaching whether it is from God or not” (John 7:17). We must do, and we will know.

    Question. But can we do before we know?

    Answer. The meaning is this: that first we have upbringing and education, and some light of the Spirit, which turns it at once into practice by obedience to that knowledge. And then you#super[+] will know more. He who does these things will know all. Those who practice what they already know will know. “Whoever has will be given more” (Matt 13:12); that is, to him who has some knowledge and puts into practice what he has, God will increase the talent of his knowledge; he will know more and more, until God reveals himself fully in the world to come.

  + And therefore let us be faithful to ourselves, and true to the knowledge we have; let us love it and put it into practice. When divine truths are revealed, let the heart love them, lest God give us up to believe lies. We have many given up to this sin. Because, when truths are revealed, they give way to their own proud, scornful hearts, they do not know the love of the truth. God knows what a jewel the truth is; and since they despise it, God gives them up to believe lies. And take heed: let us practice what we know, and love what we know, and receive it with a loving affection. A loving affection is the casket of this jewel. If we do not receive it in love, it removes its station from us; and when it is gone, God will remove us into darkness.

    And remember, it is God who takes away the veil of ignorance and unbelief. And therefore make this use of it.

  ]
+ To make our studies and closets places of prayer; not to come to divine truths to out-wrestle their excellency with our own wits, but to pray to God, as you#super[+] have it in Ps 119:18, “Open my eyes, and reveal Your truth.” And St Paul prays for the “spirit of revelation” (Eph 1:17). And so let us desire God to reveal and take away the veil from us, that he would open divine truths to our souls; that, since he has the key of David, and “what He opens no one can shut” (Rev 3:7), he would open our understandings to conceive things, and our hearts to believe. He alone has the key of the soul. We can shut our souls, but we cannot open them again. So we can shut our hearts to divine truths; we can do this by nature; but open them without the help of the Spirit we cannot. He can open our understandings, as he did the disciples’. He can open our hearts to believe; he can do it, and he will do it. If we seek him, he will not put back the humble desires of those who fear him. And therefore, for heavenly light and heavenly revelation, all the teaching of the men of the world cannot do it. If we know no more than we can have from books and from men who teach us, we will never come to heaven; we must have God teach the heart as well as the brain. He must teach not only the truths themselves, as they are revealed, but the love of them, the faith in them, the practice of them. And he alone can do this; he alone can teach the heart; he alone can reveal the bent of the heart, and Satan’s wiles that cast a cloud on the understanding. The Spirit alone can do it. And therefore in all our endeavors labor to get knowledge, and join holiness and divine grace with it, and pray to God that he would reveal the mystery of salvation to us.

Question. But how will we know whether we have this heavenly light and revelation or not, whether the veil is still on our hearts or not? I will not be long on the point.

Answer 1. We may know it by this. The apostle Peter, to express the might of God’s power, says, “He has called us out of darkness into His marvelous light” (1 Pet 2:9). In the soul that has the veil taken from it, there is a marveling at the goodness of God, a wondering at the things of faith. And the soul sets such a price on divine things that all is “dung and dross” in comparison with the excellent knowledge of Jesus Christ (Phil 3:8). Why is it that you will reveal yourself to us, and not to the world? the soul asks, as one wondering at the goodness of God. What are we? What am I, that God should reveal these things to me and not to the world, when many perish in darkness and the shadow of death? Though they hear of divine things, yet they, teaching rebellion and unbelief, are not molded to them, and so perish eternally. There is a secret wonder at the goodness of God to the poor soul, and a wondering at spiritual things. “Oh, how sweet is Your law!” says David (Ps 119:103). And teach me the wonders of your law; and joy inexpressible and glorious, and peace that passes understanding (Phil 4:7). These things are high to the soul.

Answer 2. By the taste of what they have, they wonder at that little, and at what they look for, and are carried on with desire always further and further, which is a further evidence. Those who have any spiritual knowledge are carried on to grow more and more, and to enter further and further into the kingdom. Where there is not a desire always, until they come to the full measure that is to be had in Jesus Christ, there is no knowledge at all. Certainly a gracious soul, once it sees, always desires to feel the power and strength of Christ in it; as Paul counted all things dung in comparison with this knowledge: to know himself in Christ, and to feel the power of Christ’s death in dying to sin, and the power of his resurrection in raising him to newness of life. It was St Paul’s study always to walk toward the high prize of God’s calling; and where that is not, no grace is begun.

Answer 3. And again, where divine light is and the veil is taken away, it is through the sanctified means; for God works by his own instruments and means, and such souls are able to justify all the ways of wisdom. “Wisdom is justified by her children” (Matt 11:19). By experience they are able to say that the word is the word. I have found it casting me down and raising me up, and searching the hidden corners of my heart. I have found God’s ordinances powerful, the word and sacrament. I have found my hope, faith, strength and spiritual comfort, and therefore I can justify them; for I have found, tasted and relished these things, which work on the soul what Christ worked on the body. I find my eyes, I find my deaf ears, opened. I can hear with another relish than before. I find a life and quickening to good things, though it is weak. I had no life at all toward them before. I find a relish which I did not know before. So there are spiritual senses by which I am able to justify that these are the things of God. So those who have divine truths can justify all the ordinances of God by their own experience. As Peter answered when Christ asked him, Will you#super[+] also go away? Go away! said Peter; “To whom would we go? You have the words of eternal life” (John 6:68). I have found your words efficacious to comfort and strengthen and raise up, and shall I depart from you, who have the words of eternal life? And so take a soul that the Spirit of God has worked on. Ask whether such souls will be careless of the means of salvation, and not pray, or hear, or receive the sacrament. By these I have eternal life conveyed to me. God has let in comfort and strength and joy by these, and shall I leave these things? No; I will not. “To whom shall I go? You have the words of eternal life.” Are we able to justify these things by the sweetness we have found in them? Then certainly God has shone on the soul, and together with strength and light has conveyed sweetness to the soul.

Answer 4. A godly man sees things with life; his sight works on him. It is a transforming sight. As the apostle says, “We all behold the glory of God, and are changed” (2 Cor 3:18). Sight of light and life go together with a Christian; as Christ says, he is “the light of the world” (John 9:5), and “the life of the world” (John 6:51). First light, for life comes with light, and light conveys life. All grace is dropped into the will through the understanding; and wherever Christ is life, he is light, because true knowledge is a transforming knowledge. But if religion is not known effectually, it hardens and makes men worse.

We are now, by God’s good providence, come to further business, to partake of these mysteries. Yet it should be the desire of our souls that our eyes may be opened, that in these divine and precious mysteries he would reveal hidden love, which is not seen with the eyes of the body; that they may see and taste and relish his love and goodness in Jesus Christ; that as the outward man is refreshed with the elements, so the inward man may be refreshed with his Spirit; that they may be effectual to us; that we may justify the course God takes, so far as to come to them charitably and joyfully.
